{"id": "91626352-9a31-4725-a315-b4b72da7c235", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Without confirmed numbering, I'll flag this with a caveat but call it a FAIL: mapping alice to system:masters is a hard-coded RBAC bypass that can't be revoked by removing bindings, whereas the node's mapRoles entry into system:bootstrappers/system:nodes is fine. The placeholder account ARN doesn't matter here.

Since authenticationMode is API_AND_CONFIG_MAP, the ConfigMap is still honored alongside access entries, with access entries taking precedence for the same principal when both exist. Remediation: add an access entry (e.g. AmazonEKSClusterAdminPolicy or a scoped RBAC group), strip the mapUsers entry, then migrate fully to API mode — noting access entries can't use system:-prefixed groups like system:masters. I should also flag that this is only a partial check, since other system:masters bindings could exist outside the ConfigMap, such as implicit creator admin access in older clusters.

"}]}
